   "source": [
    "## Explanation\n",
    "I chose to use a temperature of 3 and a decay of .95. My logic was that on a 4x4 grid, any change is a drastic one - so any high temperature would have us doing a lot of arbitrary back-and-forth movement.\n",
    "I would note that, starting from a random location on the grid and not moving in a direction with a negative delta, the simulated annealing agent has about a 30% chance to reach the \"10\" square, and a roughly 60% chance to hit either the 9 OR the 10 cell.\n",
    "Thus, my original idea was to have temperature decay incredibly quickly. While that did line up with the ~30-60% odds I predicted, it meant I wasn't taking as much advantage of the delta between cells.\n",
    "Having temperature decay slower means that, once I find a local maxima, I'm far less likely to stray from it. And, with my base temperature high enough (starting at 3), particularly small local maxima (such as the 3 cell) are far more likely to be ignored!\n",
    "This gave me far better results on average, settling on the 10-cell around 50% of the time, on the 9-cell around 15% of the time, and on the 3-cell around 35% of the time."
   ]
